    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d308">

                <pb facs="0482" n="388" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg388"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Olney to Mr. Mills.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-07-13"><hi rendition="#italic">July 13,
                        1896</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 140.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 162, of the 22d ultimo, inclosing copies of certain acts passed by the
                    Legislature and approved by the President of Hawaii.</p>
                <p>One of these is entitled “An act to prescribe the procedure in proceedings tor
                    naturalization of aliens,” approved June 15, 1896. One of the recited conditions
                    of this law is that an alien must set forth in his petition “that he is a
                    citizen or subject of a country having express treaty stipulations with the
                    Republic of Hawaii concerning naturalization (stating the same).”</p>
                <p>The effect of this would seem to bar, henceforth, the naturalization of citizens
                    of the United States in Hawaii, since we have no treaty of naturalization with
                    that country.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard Olney.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
